WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.080
One of the best things about special features
on DVDs and Blu-rays is the litany of deleted

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.080
scenes.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.610
But sometimes, omitting those scenes creates
pesky plot holes that would've been sealed

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.800
if only they had just stayed in place.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.990
Here are some deleted scenes that would've
fixed some of cinema's most pesky and confusing

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.900
plot points...

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:18.900
Aliens

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.869
Aliens is one of the best movies ever made,
but it does have one weird issue.

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:26.359
It seems like Ripley is barely affected by...anything.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.260
She goes through the whole movie showing about
two emotions: kill, and kill with fire.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.820
But that's a little weird, right?

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.450
After all, she comes back after a hundred
years of sleep, her whole family and life

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.520
have passed away, and she basically shrugs
it all off.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.680
It just seems like she's being a little too
cavalier about it all.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.820
But that wouldn't be the case if this deleted
scene was left in.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.320
We see here that her daughter's death of old
age really did have a profound effect on her.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.640
In addition to making Ripley not seem like,
you know, a robot, it also makes her bond

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.360
with Newt at the end of the movie make a ton
more sense: she's trying to replace the daughter-sized

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.420
hole in her heart.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.540
Harry Potter and The Deathly Hallows

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.780
If you've only ever seen the two Deathly Hallows
films and never read the books, you probably

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.060
think it's weird that the Death Eaters managed
to always find Ron, Harry, and Hermione.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.080
Were they wearing some kind of tracking beacon?

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.479
Did they forget to turn the GPS on their wands
off?

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:25.479
What the heck?

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:30.310
Well, the actual reason is simple: Voldemort
put a curse on his own name so that whenever

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:34.460
anyone says it, he and his Death Eaters can
detect them and find them.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.790
Which isn't a problem for most people, because
they all call him "You Know Who."

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.920
But then there's Harry, who refuses to.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.869
So every time he says Voldemort's name in
an attempt to puff up his chest and say "I'm

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:47.710
not scared of him," he's actually shining
a huge spotlight for all the guys who want

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:48.860
to kill him.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:52.290
Of course, eventually he learns better … because
Ron tells him.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.170
"That's how they track people now."

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:55.990
"How do you know that?"

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.479
"Overheard some bloke at the ministry talking
about it."

00:01:58.479 --> 00:02:02.049
But the filmmakers deleted that scene, so
it suddenly goes from Death Eaters knowing

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:05.420
where Harry and the gang are 24/7, to them
never knowing.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:09.030
It's not worst plot hole in the whole series,
but that scene probably could have cleared

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.200
up a whole lot of confusion.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.520
The Lord of the Rings

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:16.230
The Lord of the Rings is one of the best film
trilogies ever, but there's always been one

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.230
weird part in it: Boromir.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.490
He alternates between heroism and villainy,
seemingly at random.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:25.970
It all comes to a head when Boromir tries
to take the ring from Frodo at Amon Hen.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:30.140
Most of us assumed that the Ring's influence
corrupted Boromir, but there's a much sadder

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.620
explanation, as revealed in one of the deleted
scenes that made its way into the Extended

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:34.620
Editions.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.880
As we know from The Return of the King, Boromir's
father, Denethor, is a cruel, unforgiving

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:39.880
man.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.730
He's the guy who sends Boromir to the Council
to claim the One Ring to bring glory to Gondor.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.320
"You must go.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.660
Bring me back this mighty gift."

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.430
So when Boromir seems lustful over the Ring,
he's really just trying to be a good son to

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:57.550
please his overbearing prick of a father.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.420
"I trust this mission only to your brother.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.050
The one who will not fail me."

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.040
The guy's a total buttface!

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.819
This also means his dad has been one of the
most destructive forces in the entire saga

00:03:08.819 --> 00:03:10.120
since the first movie.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.940
Seriously, this guy is worse than Saruman.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.209
Groundhog Day

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:17.970
Groundhog Day easily ranks up there with Bill
Murray's best films.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:22.700
Murray's character relives one day in his
life, for seemingly no purpose, over and over.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:29.300
"Then put your little hand in mine, there
ain't no hill or mountain…"

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:35.140
Due to the lack of explanation in the finished
film, people have argued for years about just

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.030
what the heck the point of it all is.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:40.470
And while some might argue that's actually
one of the movie's high points, that doesn't

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.320
make the explanation's absence any less confusing.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.540
What's making Murray so death-proof?

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:47.920
"Phil!"

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:51.940
"He might be ok."

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.651
While still in development, the filmmakers
had a simple, and kind of dumb, explanation

00:03:58.651 --> 00:04:03.180
in mind: an ex of Murray's character cursed
him until he could learn how to actually love,

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.220
at which point the curse would end.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.629
Fortunately, this scene was cut before it
was even filmed, because, honestly, a clear-cut

00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:13.370
explanation like that would have just taken
away from what makes the movie so great in

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:15.180
the first place.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:15.860
"Phil?

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:16.380
Hey!

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:17.420
Phil Conners!"

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:18.260
"Ned?"

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:23.520
Star Trek

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.370
If you don't remember who Nero is, we'll catch
you up.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:32.130
He's the Romulan bald bad guy in J.J. Abrams'
2009 reboot of Star Trek who screams "Fire

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:33.130
everything!"

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:36.410
He's also the one who kills Captain Kirk's
dad, Thor, while Kirk is too busy being born

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:37.410
to save him.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.380
But after the opening scene Nero just conveniently
disappears until Kirk's all grown up.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:42.550
What was he doing?

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:43.639
Taking a vacation?

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:47.490
Actually, he was being held prisoner, off
in Deleted Scenes Land.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:51.280
The film needed about 20 years to pass so
that Nero could face off against an adult

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.050
Captain Kirk, so J.J. Abrams and company had
to give him something to do in the meantime.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:00.520
Their idea was that Nero was captured by Klingons
and held prisoner for years, until he could

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.720
finally break free.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:05.680
Listen to the voice of everybody's favorite
Star Trek character, Exposition Man:

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:11.120
"It's been more than 20 years, Nero, since
we captured your ship."

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.090
The filmmakers ultimately cut this scene out
of the movie, leaving a big, gaping question

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:18.910
mark in the plot that no amount of lens flares
could cover up.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.520
Return of the Jedi

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.930
One of the biggest questions in Return of
the Jedi has annoyed fans for years: why didn't

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:28.680
Obi-Wan just tell Luke that Darth Vader was
his father in the first place?

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.150
He started out as a pretty cool old guy, showing
him fancy laser swords in his creepy desert

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.150
hut.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.820
But then he straight up lied to Luke about
who is father really was.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:37.930
Well, guess what?

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:39.600
Turns out it wasn't Obi-Wan's fault.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.949
It was Yoda, who confessed the truth on his
deathbed.

00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:44.950
Here's how the final cut of that scene went:

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:47.560
"Not ready for the burden were you."

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:50.340
"I'm sorry."

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.780
But the original version had an extra line
that took all the blame off Obi-Wan:

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:03.320
"Not ready for the burden were you. Obi-Wan
would have told you long ago had I let him."

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:07.590
Yoda purposefully kept the secret from Luke,
even forbidding Obi-Wan from telling Luke

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.840
out of fear of what Luke would do if he knew
the truth.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.960
And while we're on the subject of confusing
Jedi dad-issues, this one's a doozy.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.330
Revenge of the Sith

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:19.639
In The Phantom Menace, we find out that Anakin
has no father.

00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:21.100
But that's a little silly.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.460
Even Jesus had a dad.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:23.509
So who was Anakin's?

00:06:23.509 --> 00:06:27.940
The Phantom Menace never tells us, but a deleted
scene from Revenge of the Sith reveals the

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:28.940
truth.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:33.070
The scene, which George Lucas outlined, but
never filmed, explains that Palpatine's master

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:36.130
taught him how to manipulate midichlorians
to create life.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:40.491
In an attempt to create the perfect pupil,
Palpatine created Anakin, saying that, from

00:06:40.491 --> 00:06:42.530
a certain point of view, he was Anakin's father.

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:46.600
The scene was still in script phase when Lucas
cut it, but it adds extra weight to Return

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:51.490
of the Jedi: Anakin, as Darth Vader, tries
to draw his son to the Dark Side, just like

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:52.569
his own "father" did.

00:06:52.569 --> 00:06:57.180
It's a cool backstory — but it still wouldn't
have made Revenge of the Sith suck any less.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:05.260
Independence Day

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:06.410
Remember Independence Day?

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:07.750
Of course you do!

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:11.729
It's a great, silly movie about humanity fighting
against aliens and defeating them with the

00:07:11.729 --> 00:07:15.610
combined powers of inspirational public speaking
and also … hacking?

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:17.169
Hold up.

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:20.690
How exactly did Jeff Goldblum get a computer
virus onto the alien ship?

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.440
It's hard enough getting a PC to play nice
with a Mac.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:26.520
Well, you guessed it: a deleted scene has
the answer.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:30.000
Remember how Goldblum found out when the aliens
were going to attack by decoding the messages

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.060
they were sending through Earth's satellites?

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.840
He used that information like a Rosetta Stone
to figure out the computers on the crashed

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:36.970
alien ship at Roswell.

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:39.160
"See these patterns here they're repeating
sequentially.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.230
Just like their countdown signal.

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:43.540
They're using that frequency for computer communications.

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:45.280
See?

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.600
That's how they're coordinating their ships."

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:51.160
From there, he figured out how to write a
virus specifically for the aliens' computers.

00:07:51.169 --> 00:07:54.789
Which still doesn't make a bunch of sense,
but it's a step up from magical hacking.

00:07:54.789 --> 00:07:57.470
So, uh, take that, aliens.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:59.740
"Welcome to earth."

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:00.740
The Goonies

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:04.069
Remember at the end of the Goonies when the
kids are screaming at their parents about

00:08:04.069 --> 00:08:05.330
all their adventures?

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:07.440
Out of the blue, Data starts talking about
an octopus:

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:09.200
"What happened out there? Were your lives in danger?"

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.289
"The octopus was very scary."

00:08:11.289 --> 00:08:12.569
"Octopus?"

00:08:12.569 --> 00:08:16.210
And no, you didn't forget about the epic scene
where the Goonies fight an octopus, because

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:17.979
it was taken out of the final cut.

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:19.710
But the scene did survive.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:22.860
When the Goonies are swimming through the
lake to the pirate ship, they run into a giant

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:27.289
octopus that tries to eat them all, because
what's more pirate-y than an octopus-infested

00:08:27.289 --> 00:08:28.289
lake?

00:08:28.289 --> 00:08:29.289
Nothing, that's what.

00:08:29.289 --> 00:08:32.680
So get your truffle shuffle on, because this
mystery is solved!

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:34.480
Thanks for watching!

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:37.080
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.510
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

